WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.160
[Piano instrumental]

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:05.440
Just five years ago,

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.640
it was hard to imagine

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.880
socialists winning elections in America.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.120
It's the United States,

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:12.800
the global heart of capitalism.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.400
But for a long time,

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.920
socialism was a dirty word.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.080
Republicans called everyone from

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.120
Harry Truman, to Bill Clinton a "socialist."

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.000
In a few short years, everything has changed.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.920
A lifelong democratic socialist

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.720
almost became the Democratic presidential nominee

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:29.220
*twice.*

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.880
Polls show socialism is soaring in popularity,

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.120
and a huge number of young, socialist

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.920
people of color are getting elected to office.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.080
Socialists like Jamal Bowman in New York,

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.240
and Corey Bush in Missouri are defeating

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.840
incumbent congresspeople.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.760
Black and brown working-class socialists

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:47.760
are winning seats in state legislatures

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.040
and city councils.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.200
And the biggest name in the Democratic Party

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.922
is a latina socialist from the Bronx.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:55.600
If you're excited about

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.000
creating a society that works for everyone,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.680
you might be inspired by socialists'

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:02.480
big victories in 2018 and 2020.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.000
And you might be wondering if

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.680
you too can become a voice for

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:06.527
your community.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.384
That's what i'm here to talk about.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.160
I teach math at a middle school

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:11.600
in Crown Heights Brooklyn.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:12.960
I'm an activist, and a member

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.440
of the Democratic Socialist of America,

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.840
and in 2020 I became the first

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.800
Black gay man to be elected&nbsp;a&nbsp;
State Senator in New York

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.200
It wasn't easy.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.720
In the Democratic primary,

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.560
I ran against a state assemblyperson

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.320
who was endorsed by the incumbent.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.720
I was opposed by the local political machine,

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.000
but in the end we won with

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.200
58% of the vote.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.120
It took a lot of hard work,

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.920
but we won, and I want to help you

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:38.640
do the same thing.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.040
So, here are some things to consider

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.360
if you want to run for office as a socialist.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.120
First: Know *why* you're running for office.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.720
Most people who run for office,

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.640
either Democrat or Republican,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.240
run because they have a big ego

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.840
or they want a career in politics

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.080
and it's easy to feel like they're the ones who

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:56.960
belong in office.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.440
But socialists have a different approach.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.360
Socialism stresses community and solidarity.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.640
You can run for office to help

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:05.920
people in your community.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:06.800
People you know.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.080
People you encounter every day

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:09.280
at work, or in the neighborhood.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.840
In my class i had one girl

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.680
who was tired all the time.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.200
and when I checked in it was because

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.640
she was up late because she had to

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.240
sleep at her grandma's house.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.240
It was obvious she didn't have a secure place

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.040
to sleep every night.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:22.960
How could she learn when she didn't

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:24.480
have her basic needs met?

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.240
Another student of mine was struggling

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.720
and then one day just stopped showing up.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.120
A few days later I got an email from his mom

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:32.560
saying they had to move to Long Island

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.000
because they were evicted.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.280
I ran for office because&nbsp;of&nbsp;experiences like these.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.480
I ran to help people like my&nbsp;students and their families.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:42.960
I knew what our system was doing to people,

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.360
and how it impacted the ability of my students

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.800
to give their all in the classroom.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.120
That leads me to my second point:

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.600
We need more working class socialists in office

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.680
But, you don't have to&nbsp;
start by running for office.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.520
I started out by campaigning&nbsp;
for&nbsp;same-sex marriage in New York

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.840
I organized rallies for Black Lives Matter.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.840
I canvas for Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.240
I worked on access to affordable housing

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.160
with the Democratic Socialists of America.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.480
I wasn't new to these struggles.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.360
By the time I ran for office, I'd been fighting for years,

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.520
and I was part of a broader movement for justice

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.440
Movements are made up of activists and organizers,

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.600
and the best campaigns are powered by people.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.480
You can do an incredible amount of good by

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.160
Working as a tenant organizer

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.240
or contributing to union organization campaigns.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.480
Even though the media likes to focus on

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.720
the big electoral victories,

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:34.800
the real progress comes from thousands

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.280
of small victories fought for by people

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.640
you might never hear about.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.040
Over a thousand people volunteered

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.720
on my campaign for state senate

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.320
and they are the reason we won.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.280
If you do want to run for office,

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.040
consider where you are most needed

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:51.120
there are a ton of really important positions

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.200
in local or state politics that don't get

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.480
as much attention as senate

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:55.760
or congressional races

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.520
but are incredibly influential.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.880
Judgeships, city or town council seats

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.960
state legislatures, school boards

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:04.640
positions at the county level

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.840
So much power is held in offices with elections

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.440
that go uncontested.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:11.120
You'd be surprised how far you can go!

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:14.720
Third: focus on the material issues that

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:16.320
shape the lives of your constituents

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.520
Socialism is about material change

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:21.840
helping people in real, tangible ways

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:23.360
with programs and policies that

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:24.880
ensure everyone has access

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:26.000
to free health care,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:26.720
decent housing,

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:27.840
and a good education.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.120
Don't run on technocratic tweaks,

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.264
be ambitious!

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.560
Campaign on systems that ensure people

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.880
have what they need to survive, and thrive.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.360
Talk about the issues you've experienced

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.080
in your community.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:41.840
Is rent too expensive?

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.880
Do nurses and doctors not have enough funding?

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.360
Do local schools not have enough money?

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:50.160
Socialism is also about power.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.160
Who has it, and who doesn't.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.240
That's why socialist candidates

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.840
need to run on structural solutions,

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:57.920
finding ways to bring power

00:04:57.920 --> 00:04:59.120
back to ordinary people

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:00.560
Talk with people in

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:01.840
your community every day

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.360
about the problems they face

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.040
and the future you want to build together

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:07.520
your single biggest advantage

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:08.320
is your community.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.000
And fourth: if you lose

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.280
don't be afraid to try again.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.080
In 2017, I ran for city council

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:16.880
and came up short.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.600
It was tough, but a few years later

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.440
I tried again running for state senate

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.360
and won, so don't be discouraged

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:23.860
if you don't win.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:27.680
In 2018 Corey Bush ran for Congress in St. Louis

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:30.080
and lost to a 20-year incumbent.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:32.400
Two years later, she came back and won.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.120
Socialists are winning elections because

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.480
we're focused on creating a&nbsp;
system that works for everyone,

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:39.920
and because we recognize that

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:41.840
the system today is designed to

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:44.960
benefit only a small, wealthy minority.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:46.240
Socialists are winning because

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.480
we're building power by working together.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.880
Day after day, and people

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.160
across the country are on board.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:54.000
No matter where you live,

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.760
there are people all around you

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:57.120
eager for change and

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:59.680
seeking practical, tangible solutions

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:01.040
to the injustices they face.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:03.680
So my last piece of advice is this:

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:05.840
remember that you are not alone

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:07.920
if you're thinking about

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.600
running for office as a socialist

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:10.880
look for other organizing groups

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:12.160
in your area and online.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.760
Together we can create a better society,

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.040
one that works for all of us

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:19.760
I'm Jabari Brisport,

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:22.718
New York state senator, for the Gravel Institute

00:06:22.718 --> 00:06:26.161
[Techno music plays]

00:06:26.161 --> 00:06:31.952
Thank you to our patrons for making all this possible 

00:06:31.952 --> 00:07:06.092
[Techno music continues]

